The finite-time-blowup test problem (doc/dfmm_3d.md Sec. 7, Stage 5), from
Downloads/before_blowup_ideal_gas_pedagogical.pdf.

Compile:
  cd bin && make COMPILER=METAL NDIM=3 HYDRO=1 DFMM=2 INIT=13 ramses   (dfmm)
  cd bin && make COMPILER=METAL NDIM=3 HYDRO=1        INIT=13 ramses   (hydro)
Both builds read this namelist; the DFMM_PARAMS block is simply ignored when
the binary was compiled with DFMM=0.  Do not write an ampersand-prefixed group
name anywhere in this header: gfortran's namelist reader will treat it as the
start of that group and then fail on the surrounding prose.

Axisymmetric straining flow, exactly divergence-free and periodic, with the
strain of the note's construction at the box centre:
    S = (1/Delta) diag(-2,-2,4),  ||S||_op = 4/Delta,  t_def = Delta/4.
The strain tensor is diagonal EVERYWHERE in this field -- no shear anywhere.
See hydro/condinit.f90, #if INIT==BLOWUP, for the field and its properties.

Calibration.  In code units boxlen=rho0=p0=1 and gamma=5/3, so c_s=1.2910 and
mu = p0*dfmm_tau = dfmm_tau.  The note's decisive threshold is
    Delta_P = 8 mu / p_g = 8 dfmm_tau,
and the Newtonian extrapolation at the box centre has smallest eigenvalue
    lam_min(P_NS)/p = 1 - 8 dfmm_tau / Delta,
so the whole problem is governed by the single ratio 8*dfmm_tau/Delta.

Strain and Mach number are linked: the peak speed is 2C/k = 4/(Delta*k) with
k = 2 pi blowup_nwave / L, so a large strain at fixed box means a large Mach
number unless blowup_nwave is raised (which costs resolution).  The ladder
below therefore FIXES the flow -- Delta = 1.644 gives Mach 0.300 -- and sweeps
dfmm_tau, which is legitimate because only the ratio matters:

  8 tau/Delta   dfmm_tau   t_c/t_def    Kn      lam_min(P_NS)/p   regime
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
      0.1        0.02055     0.040     ~0.13         +0.90    NS clearly adequate
      0.4        0.08220     0.160     ~0.53         +0.60    marginal
      1.0        0.20550     0.400     ~1.33          0.00    NS at the cone edge
      2.0        0.41100     0.800     ~2.67         -1.00    NS inadmissible

(t_c = 0.8*dfmm_tau from the hard-sphere relation mu/p_g = (5/4) t_c;
 Kn ~ t_c c_s k.)  Note what the table shows: the note's check 7 can only fire
when t_c is comparable to t_def, i.e. at Knudsen number of order unity.  The
Navier-Stokes breakdown it identifies is a straightforwardly kinetic one, not a
subtle high-Reynolds effect.

Run to a few deformation times: t_def = 0.4110, so tout = 1.0 is 2.4 t_def.

&RUN_PARAMS
hydro=.true.
ncontrol=10
nrestart=0
nremap=0
nsubcycle=10*1
verbose=.false.
nstepmax=100000
nsuperoct=2
/

&AMR_PARAMS
levelmin=5
levelmax=5
ngridtot=2000000
ncachemax=1000000
nexpand=1
boxlen=1.0
/

&INIT_PARAMS
nregion=1
region_type(1)='square'
x_center=0.5
y_center=0.5
z_center=0.5
length_x=10.0
length_y=10.0
length_z=10.0
exp_region=10.0
d_region=1.0
u_region=0.0
v_region=0.0
w_region=0.0
p_region=1.0
/

&BLOWUP_PARAMS
blowup_delta=1.644
blowup_rho0=1.0
blowup_p0=1.0
blowup_nwave=1
blowup_init_ns=.false.
/

&OUTPUT_PARAMS
foutput=0
tout=1.0
/

&HYDRO_PARAMS
gamma=1.6666666666666667
courant_factor=0.8
slope_type=1
riemann='hll'
/

&DFMM_PARAMS
dfmm_tau=0.20550
dfmm_prandtl=0.6666666666666667
dfmm_source=.true.
dfmm_diag=.true.
dfmm_fatal_realizability=.false.
/
